<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.1.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- eldato harness apply, the SAME pass the Linux cells run on a systemd timer (#2416).
         Scheduled here by scripts/install-harness-apply-timer.sh, which renders this file and
         bootstraps it. ONE install verb, the platform's own scheduler (#2058).

         WHY THIS FILE IS NOT IN templates/launchd/. scripts/install-launchd.sh installs EVERY
         templates/launchd/*.plist with NO arguments and a different substitution set, and
         pi-bootstrap/setup.sh runs it on every Darwin box. A copy there would render with its
         placeholders unresolved, be SKIPPED with a warning, and the box would silently have no
         scheduled pass while the installer printed nothing wrong. The subdirectory keeps it out of
         that glob and out of templates/launchd/RETIRED (which errors on a label that has both).

         NO PLACEHOLDER MAY APPEAR INSIDE AN XML COMMENT. This comment is XML text: a doubled dash
         is forbidden in it, so a placeholder that renders an option flag would make the installed
         plist not parse, launchd would refuse it, and the box would silently have no pass. That is
         why this block names placeholders without braces, and why do_install_launchd refuses a
         rendered plist that does not parse.

         WHY THE ENTRY POINT IS THE CHECKOUT'S OWN PYTHON, NOT bash. macOS TCC is per-binary: a
         launchd-spawned bash cannot read a script under ~/Documents and a launchd-spawned git
         cannot read a repo there, so a pass whose whole job is git against that checkout would
         fail closed every ten minutes while launchctl list showed a healthy job. The interpreter
         that holds the access reads the script and hands it to bash on stdin (the same launcher
         install-disk-reaper-timer.sh uses). No usable interpreter is a REFUSAL at install time.

         TEN MINUTES, matching the systemd timer. The pass is a NO-OP when the pin already equals
         the ref tip, so a converged box pays one fetch per tick.

         No StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath: the pass writes its own record and log under
         HARNESS_APPLY_STATE_DIR, and an unbounded launchd-captured log is the symlink-truncation
         surface the reapers already guard. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>{{LABEL}}</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>{{PY_BIN}}</string>
        <string>{{LAUNCHER}}</string>
        <string>--script</string>
        <string>{{PASS}}</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>{{HOME}}</string>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <!-- ONE record, one path: the scheduled pass and an operator's status verb read the same
             file. (An option flag is spelled without its dashes here on purpose: a doubled dash is
             forbidden inside an XML comment, and writing one makes this plist unparseable.) -->
        <key>HARNESS_APPLY_STATE_DIR</key>
        <string>{{STATE_DIR}}</string>
        <!-- The checkout is PINNED, never resolved from the job's environment: a launchd job inherits
             none of a login shell's rc files, so leaving it ambient would converge a different tree
             and say so nowhere. -->
        <key>HARNESS_APPLY_REPO</key>
        <string>{{PASS_REPO}}</string>
        <key>HARNESS_APPLY_REF</key>
        <string>{{PASS_REF}}</string>
        <!-- An empty value is meaningful: the pass decides pin-vs-sync from the box's own posture. -->
        <key>HARNESS_APPLY_MODE</key>
        <string>{{PASS_MODE}}</string>
    </dict>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>{{INTERVAL_SEC}}</integer>
    <!-- A pass AT LOAD as well as on the interval, so a freshly installed agent has a record within
         seconds instead of after the first interval. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <!-- A background convergence, never a deadline: it must not compete with a lane for CPU or I/O. -->
    <key>Nice</key>
    <integer>10</integer>
    <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
    <true/>
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
